## keyof — the union of property names

```ts
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

type UserKey = keyof User; // 'id' | 'name' | 'age'
```

`keyof T` gives a union of `T`'s keys.

## Indexed access — the union of value types

Index a type with a key (or a union of keys) to get the value type. Index with `keyof T` to get all value types:

```ts
type UserValue = User[keyof User]; // string | number
type NameType  = User['name'];     // string
```

## Lifting a value into a type with typeof

For a runtime object — common with an `as const` config — `typeof` turns the value into a type first:

```ts
const ROUTES = {
  home: '/',
  about: '/about',
  blog: '/blog',
} as const;

type RouteKey  = keyof typeof ROUTES;                  // 'home' | 'about' | 'blog'
type RoutePath = (typeof ROUTES)[keyof typeof ROUTES]; // '/' | '/about' | '/blog'
```

This derives the union from a single source of truth instead of maintaining a parallel `type` by hand.

## Why it matters

It is the foundation of generic, key-safe code:

```ts
function get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}
```

The return type `T[K]` tracks exactly which key you asked for — `get(user, 'age')` is typed `number`, not `string | number`. `keyof` plus indexed access is what makes a generic accessor precise instead of lossy.

